WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.712
Here is a simple Spark notebook workflow.

00:00:02.712 --> 00:00:05.227
A file is read into a DataFrame.

00:00:05.227 --> 00:00:09.117
The DataFrame is registered as a temporary view.

00:00:09.117 --> 00:00:11.672
Then Spark SQL queries that view.

00:00:11.672 --> 00:00:17.772
This code is not meant for the browser practice engine because it requires a real Spark session.

00:00:17.772 --> 00:00:23.752
The important idea is the connection between files, DataFrames, temporary views, and SQL.

00:00:23.752 --> 00:00:30.022
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:30.022 --> 00:00:34.377
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:34.377 --> 00:00:38.772
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:44.982
In a real setting such as a healthcare analytics platform processing large longitudinal event tables,

00:00:44.982 --> 00:00:51.007
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:51.007 --> 00:00:53.162
before discussing syntax.

00:00:53.162 --> 00:00:59.316
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
